1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
My topic is handedness-whether in different sports it is better to be left

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,958
or right-sided or whether a more balanced approach is more successful,

3
00:00:10,958 --> 00:00:18,141
I'm left-handed myself and I actually didn't see any relevance to my own life

4
00:00:18,281 --> 00:00:24,200
when I happened to start reading an article by a sports psychologist called Peter Matthews

5
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:30,257
He spent the first part of the article talking about handedness in music instead of sport,

6
00:00:30,257 --> 00:00:34,800
which I have to say almost put me off from reading further.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:40,422
But what I soon became struck by was the sheer volume of both observation

8
00:00:40,422 --> 00:00:44,036
and investigation he had done in many different sports

9
00:00:44,036 --> 00:00:50,060
and I felt persuaded that what he had to say would be of real interest.

10
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:52,816
I think Matthews' findings will be beneficial,

11
00:00:52,816 --> 00:00:57,542
not so much in helping sportspeople to work on their weaker side,

12
00:00:57,542 --> 00:01:04,630
but more that they can help them identify the most suitable strategies to use in a given game.

13
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:08,360
Although most trainers know how important handedness is,

14
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:16,285
at present they are rather reluctant to make use of the insights scientists like Matthews can give,

15
00:01:16,285 --> 00:01:24,210
which I think is rather short-sighted because focusing on individual flexibility is only part of the story.

16
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:27,130
Anyway, back to the article.

17
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:32,940
Matthews found a German study which looked at what he called 'mixed-handedness',

18
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:38,750
that is, the capacity to use both left and right hands equally.

19
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:44,560
It looked at mixed-handedness in 40 musicians on a variety of instruments.

20
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:48,496
Researchers examined a number of variables, e.

21
00:01:48,496 --> 00:01:51,220
g. type of instrument played

22
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:56,930
regularity of practice undertaken and length of time playing instrument...

23
00:01:56,930 --> 00:02:03,115
and found the following: keyboard players had high levels of mixed-handedness,

24
00:02:03,115 --> 00:02:09,300
whereas string players like cellists and violinists strongly favoured one hand.

25
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:14,340
Also those who started younger were more mixed-handed.

26
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:20,000
Matthews also reports studies of handedness in apes.

27
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,040
Apes get a large proportion of their food by 'fishing' ants from ant hills.

28
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:33,245
The studies show that apes, like humans, show handedness-though for them right-

29
00:02:33,245 --> 00:02:40,450
and left-handedness is about equal, whereas about 85% of humans are right-handed.

30
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:50,730
Studies showed that apes consistently using the same hand fished out 30% more ants than those varying between the two.

31
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:56,180
Matthews started researching several different sports

32
00:02:56,180 --> 00:03:00,000
and found different types of handedness in each.

33
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,480
By the way, he uses 'handedness' to refer to the dominant side for feet

34
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,720
and eyes as well as hands.

35
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:11,052
Anyway, his team measured the hand,

36
00:03:11,052 --> 00:03:13,220
feet and eyes of 2

37
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:21,047
611 players and found that there were really three main types of laterality:

38
00:03:21,047 --> 00:03:26,601
mixed. You work equally well on both sides-both hand and eye;

39
00:03:26,601 --> 00:03:34,930
single. You tend to favour one side but both hand and eye favour the same side;

40
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:41,080
and cross-laterality-a player's hands and eyes favour only one side

41
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:43,260
but they are opposite sides.

42
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:45,590
Let's start with hockey.

43
00:03:45,590 --> 00:03:50,312
Matthews found that it was best to be mixed-handed. This is

44
00:03:50,312 --> 00:03:55,120
because a hockey stick must be deployed in two directions

45
00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:00,700
It would be a drawback to have hand or eye favouring one side.

46
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:10,450
An interesting finding is that mixed-handed hockey players were significantly more confident than their single-handed counterparts.

47
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:14,882
Things are slightly different in racket sports like tennis.

48
00:04:14,882 --> 00:04:22,760
Here the important thing is to have the dominant hand and eye on the one side.

49
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:27,380
This means that there is a bigger area of vision on the side

50
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:29,540
where most of the action occurs.

51
00:04:29,540 --> 00:04:31,940
If a player is cross lateral

52
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:36,695
the racket is invisible from the dominant eye for much of the swing,

53
00:04:36,695 --> 00:04:41,140
it means that they can only make corrections much later

54
00:04:41,140 --> 00:04:43,850
and often the damage has been done by then.

55
00:04:43,850 --> 00:04:47,560
And moving to a rather different type of sport

56
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:52,170
which involves large but precise movements gymnastics.

57
00:04:52,170 --> 00:04:56,840
It's been found that cross hand-eye favouring is best.

58
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:59,523
The predominant reason for this is

59
00:04:59,523 --> 00:05:06,230
because it aids balance which is of course absolutely central to performance in this sport.
